<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Brazilian Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1930 CE – 1937 CE · capital Rio de Janeiro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The years of Getúlio Vargas's rise after the Revolution of 1930, when Brazil centralised power, gave women the vote and drifted towards dictatorship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Revolution of 1930 ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-brazilian-republic">First Brazilian Republic</a> and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> as head of a provisional government. He dissolved Congress and the state legislatures and appointed interventors to govern the states, breaking the power of the old oligarchies.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Vargas</a> created a Ministry of Labour and began social legislation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutionalist-revolution">Constitutionalist Revolution</a> of 1932, which was defeated. The constitution of 1934 confirmed women's right to vote, granted by the Electoral Code of 1932, and introduced corporatist representation, and Congress elected Vargas president. Politics polarised between the fascist-inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-integralist-action">Integralists</a> and the left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-alliance">National Liberation Alliance</a>, which launched a failed communist uprising in 1935.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Citing an invented communist plot, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohen-plan">Cohen Plan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Vargas</a> staged a coup on 10 November 1937, closing Congress and creating the authoritarian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/estado-novo-brazil">Estado Novo (Brazil)</a>. The 1934 constitution, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> third, had lasted barely three years.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-brazilian-republic.jpg" alt="Second Brazilian Republic" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Raimundo Teixeira Mendes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brazil_(1889%E2%80%931960).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1930 CE – 1937 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rio de Janeiro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Getúlio Vargas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vargas Era (1930-1937)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=second-brazilian-republic" aria-label="Second Brazilian Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3525 3023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Brazilian Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3845 3279L3845 3279M3845 3279L3845 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3845 3279h0M3845 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3845 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3845 3279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rio de Janeiro. <a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=second-brazilian-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
